bset and main list can contain **nested lists of arbitrary depth**, and elements can be primitive (e.g., integers or strings) or nested lists themselves.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ **Core Assumptions**\n- **Subsets are defined as:** Every element of the subset must be **present at any level** of nesting within the main list. That is, the **element exists in the main list**, either directly or as part of a subnested list.\n- **Element equality is strict:** Two lists are considered the same only if they are **structurally identical** (i.e., both in content and depth).\n- **Empty subset:** An empty list is a subset of any list (as it has no elements to verify).\n- **Empty main list:** A non-empty subset cannot be a subset of an empty list.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ **Approach Overview**\nWe define a **recursive `contains` helper function** that performs a depth-first traversal of the main list. It checks whether a target **element** exists at **any level of nesting** inside the main list.\n\nThen, we use this helper function in the main `is_subset` function to verify that **each element of the subset** is present in the main list.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ **Python Implementation**\n\n```python\ndef is_subset(subset, main):\n    def contains(element, main_list):\n        for item in main_list:\n            if item == element:\n                return True\n            if isinstance(item, list):\n                if contains(element, item):\n                    return True\n        return False\n\n    for element in subset:\n        if not contains(element, main):\n            return False\n    return True\n```\n\n---\n\n### ✅ **Example Usage**\n\n```python\nprint(is_subset([1, [2, 3]], [[1, 2], [2, 3]]))  # True\nprint(is_subset([1, [2, 3]], [1, [2, 4]]))       # False\nprint(is_subset([], [1, 2]))                     # True\nprint(is_subset([1, [2]], [[1, 2], [3, 4]]))     # True\nprint(is_subset([[1, 2]], [[1, 2, 3], [[1, 2]]])) # True\nprint(is_subset([3], [[1], [2, 3]]))             # True\n```\n\