orrect.\n\nSo the code works.\n\nThus, the code is correct.\n</think>\n\nTo solve the problem of finding the **sum of all odd-length subarrays** of a given array efficiently, we employ a mathematical approach rather than explicitly generating all subarrays. This ensures we maintain an **O(n)** time complexity, which is optimal and suitable even for large arrays.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ **Approach Overview**\n\nEach element in the array contributes to a number of subarrays of odd length. The task is to calculate the **total contribution** of each element and then sum them up.\n\nKey insight:\n\n- A subarray of odd length includes exactly **odd number** of elements.\n- For any given element at position `i`, it will appear in multiple odd-length subarrays. We can compute how many such subarrays include it by analyzing the number of valid start and end positions that result in odd-length subarrays.\n\n---\n\n### 🔍 **Mathematical Strategy**\n\nFor each element at index `i`, we calculate:\n\n- How many **even-length** (left) ranges (from 0 to `i`) exist and how many **odd-length** (left) ranges exist — that is:\n  - `count_even_left = (i // 2) + 1`\n  - `count_odd_left = (i + 1) - count_even_left`\n\n- How many **even-length** and **odd-length** (right) ranges (from `i` to `n - 1`) exist:\n  - `count_even_right = (end // 2) - ((start - 1) // 2)`\n  - `count_odd_right = ((end + 1) // 2) - (start // 2)`\n\n- The number of subarrays of **odd length** that include the element at `i` is:\n  - `total_pairs = count_even_left * count_even_right + count_odd_left * count_odd_right`\n\n- This count is then multiplied by the value of the element and added to the total sum.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ **Python Implementation**\n\n```python\ndef sum_odd_length_subarrays(arr):\n    n = len(arr)\n    total = 0\n    for i in range(n):\n        # Count even and odd ranges on the left\n        count_even_left = (i // 2) + 1\n        count_odd_left = (i + 1) - count_even_left\n        \n        # Count even and odd ranges on the right\n        start = i\n        end = n - 1\n        count_even_right = (end // 2) - ((start - 1) // 2)\n        count_odd_right = ((end + 1) // 2) - (start // 2)\n        \n        # Total odd-length subarrays including arr[i]\n        total_pairs = count_even_left * count_even_right + count_odd_left * count_odd_right\n        \n        # Add contribution of arr[i] to the total sum\n        total += arr[i] * total_pairs\n    \n    return total\n```\n\n---\n\n### ❗ Example Usage\n\n```python\narr = [1, 2, 3]\nprint(sum_odd_length_subarrays(arr))  # Output: 12\n\narr = [1, 2, 3, 4]\nprint(sum_odd_length_subarrays(arr))  # Output: 25\n\narr = [2, 3, 4]\nprint(sum_odd_length_subarrays(arr))  # Output: 18\n```\n\n---\n\n### ✅ **Efficiency**\n\n- **Time Complexity:** `O(n)`\n- **Space Complexity:** `O(1)` (constant extra space)\n\nThis solution is optimized and avoids the inefficiency of enumerating all subarrays, which would be `O(n^2)` in the worst case."}
